{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "All 10 Pareto points are `iterative`; the other families are strictly worse on the area axis (best feasible area: iterative 7806 um^2, unrolled_k 9607, pipelined_m 24396, pipelined 47147). Under a min-area objective with only a >=1 MSPS / <=2^-10 error constraint, the feasible throughput is met everywhere (best seen 201 MSPS) and the error constraint is met by the whole front (worst front point 2^-11.78), so both the min-area pick and the hypervolume live in the iterative family. The front is nevertheless only a thin slice of the trade-off: data_width spans 16..20 of the registry's 8..28, and accuracy_bits spans 11.8..16.6 against an HV accuracy reference of 10, so the staircase's top end is truncated at 16.6 bits while nothing below area 7806 was found feasible. Both the low-area end (smaller data_width with enough n_iter/guard bits, error floor of ~2^-(W-1) still leaves headroom to the 2^-10 limit) and the high-accuracy end (larger data_width/n_iter) are unexplored, and each end carries large HV weight because the area reference is 40000. A full-range NSGA-II over the on-front family, seeded with the current front, is therefore the highest-value use of this round; refinement around the current narrow band would only re-sample the same region."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Spend the final 100-eval round mapping the front over the full ranges of the families on it. The spec selection rule is min area_um2, and every Pareto design is `iterative` (best feasible areas elsewhere are unrolled_k 9607, pipelined_m 24396, pipelined 47147, so the other families cannot win the area objective and need no further refinement). The front currently spans accuracy_bits 11.8..23.9, but the binding accuracy constraint is only 2^-10 (~10 bits) and the front's lowest-accuracy point is 11.8 bits — the mandatory region between 10 and 11.8 bits is entirely unmapped, and the feasible data_width range on the front (15..28, registry min 8) shows smaller/less-accurate designs than the current best (iterative dw=16 N=23, area 7806, 11.8 bits) are reachable, which is exactly how the area axis gets pushed below 7806 and the HV reference plane at area 40000 is better covered. The right end also has headroom (max accuracy seen 23.89 bits vs registry data_width up to 28). With HV still improving (+8.9% last round) and the front covering only part of the area/accuracy range, a seeded NSGA-II sweep over the full iterative (and, for completeness, the other feasible on-front) ranges is the highest-value use of the reserved budget."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Final round and the full 400-eval budget is spent, so the run ends here. The spec is feasible with margin: 0% of evaluations violate throughput (best seen 201 MSPS vs >= 1 required) and only 16% violate the error bound, with the best design at 2.74e-08 (2^-25.12) vs <= 2^-10. The feasible Pareto front has 31 designs covering the whole useful range: area_um2 7701..15684 against the HV reference 40000, and accuracy_bits 11.4..25.1 against the reference 10 (data_width 16..28 on the front, i.e. a broad slice of the 8..28 registry range). All four families were explored and the front is purely `iterative`; the faster families (pipelined 201 MSPS, pipelined_m 66.8 MSPS, unrolled_k 8.88 MSPS) are all far larger in area (best feasible 47147 / 24396 / 9607 um^2), so they are off the min-area front for this spec, and `map_front` is no longer available. Hypervolume did improve (+8.2% this round) but the budget is exhausted, so no further refinement is possible. The selection rule (min area_um2) picks iterative [data_width=17 n_iter=17 angle_guard=-1 frac_guard=0 rounding=trunc] at area 7701 um^2 with accuracy 11.4 bits (> 10 required) and 6.8 MSPS (> 1 required), which satisfies every constraint, so the outcome is `stop` rather than `infeasible`."
}
